'''Open-source intelligence''' (OSINT) is the collection and analysis of publicly or commercially accessible information to answer a defined intelligence question. It differs from covert acquisition and from merely collecting links.

== Collection and analysis ==
Define a legitimate question, scope and source criteria. Collect lawfully accessible material, preserve its provenance and distinguish original evidence from republications. Assess source independence, temporal relevance, context and possible manipulation. The Berkeley Protocol provides professional guidance for digital open-source investigations, particularly international human rights and criminal-law contexts; its specific legal purpose should not be conflated with all intelligence work.

== Verification and uncertainty ==
A reproducible record may include original URL, capture timestamp, checksum of a preserved copy and a documented chain of handling. A checksum confirms byte integrity, not authenticity or truth. Compare independently sourced evidence and seek disconfirming observations. Multiple accounts repeating one initial report are not independent corroboration. Make alternative explanations explicit and mark unresolved provenance.

== Ethics and practical boundaries ==
Public accessibility does not eliminate privacy, safety, consent or legal constraints. Minimize unnecessary collection of personal information, restrict sensitive evidence access and avoid unauthorized access. Analysts should distinguish an evidentiary finding from a confidence judgment and explain what could change their conclusion. The protocol's recommendations for evidence preservation do not authorize unrestricted surveillance.

